From: Feiyang Yu <2541290956@qq.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: stmmac: drop host TX in flight when the NSS firmware claims the GMAC

qca_dwmac_dp_claim() stops the host DMA with frames possibly still on the
host TX ring. They never complete, so BQL keeps their bytes in flight. If
the queue limit is still small - right after a link came up, when the
limit starts at zero - the stack has set __QUEUE_STATE_STACK_XOFF, and the
netif_tx_wake_all_queues() at the end of the claim only clears the driver
bit. The queue stays stopped, the owner's xmit is never called, and the
TX watchdog fires five seconds later:

  eth1: qca-dwmac: data plane claimed
  eth1: NETDEV WATCHDOG: CPU: 1: transmit queue 0 timed out 5740 ms
  eth1: Reset adapter.

On a DSA conduit the reset closes all user ports, which nothing reopens:
the LAN is dead until reboot. Seen on an RTL8367S board (H3C Magic
RC3000 v3) whose LAN port linked up 50 ms before the conduit was claimed
at boot; reproduced every time by claiming with BQL limit_max set to 0
under a ping flood.

Free the stale frames, clear the descriptors and reset the queue, as
stmmac_tx_err() does, before handing the queue to the owner.

Signed-off-by: Feiyang Yu <2541290956@qq.com>
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c
@@ -2418,6 +2418,23 @@
 	stmmac_stop_all_dma(priv);
 
 	/*
+	 * Frames the host queued just before the stop never complete now: the
+	 * DMA is stopped and NAPI will not clean them. Their bytes would stay
+	 * in flight in BQL, and a queue whose limit is still small (the first
+	 * frames after the link came up) keeps __QUEUE_STATE_STACK_XOFF set,
+	 * which netif_tx_wake_all_queues() below does not clear. The stack
+	 * then never calls the owner's xmit, and five seconds later the TX
+	 * watchdog resets the adapter - on a DSA conduit that closes every
+	 * user port. Drop them the way stmmac_tx_err() does, which also keeps
+	 * the host ring empty for the bounce that follows a release.
+	 */
+	for (u32 queue = 0; queue < priv->plat->tx_queues_to_use; queue++) {
+		dma_free_tx_skbufs(priv, &priv->dma_conf, queue);
+		stmmac_clear_tx_descriptors(priv, &priv->dma_conf, queue);
+		stmmac_reset_tx_queue(priv, queue);
+	}
+
+	/*
 	 * The firmware enables the DMA interrupts for itself and the line is
 	 * shared with the host's GIC: left unmasked, the host takes one
 	 * interrupt per DMA event it has nothing to do for (tens of thousands
